* ============================================================================
* Detection: Hidden Instructions in Email Body (Indirect Injection Payload)
* ============================================================================
* Author:   Daniel Rodriguez  (github.com/danielrodriguez-sec)
* Project:  Indirect Prompt Injection via Email
*
* Purpose:  Flag inbound emails whose body contains text concealed from human
*           readers but ingested by an AI email assistant. Indirect prompt
*           injection hides instructions where the human does not look and the
*           AI does. This is the primary (input-side) detection - catch the
*           payload before the agent processes it.
*
* Signal:   Two payload indicators in the raw email body:
*             (1) CSS/markup concealment - display:none, visibility:hidden,
*                 color-matched text (white-on-white), sub-2px font, zero-width
*                 characters (U+200B/U+200D/U+FEFF);
*             (2) injection-phrase signatures - "ignore previous instructions",
*                 "do not summarize", "system notice", "disregard the ... task".
*           Concealment alone or a phrase alone raises suspicion; both together
*           in one body is high-confidence.
*
* Framework mapping:
*   OWASP LLM01:2025  - Prompt Injection (indirect variant)
*   MITRE ATLAS       - AML.T0051.001 (LLM Prompt Injection: Indirect)
*
* Fields expected (adapt to your pipeline):
*   body           - the raw email body (HTML source, pre-render), NOT the
*                    rendered text - concealment lives in the markup
*   sender, subject, message_id - for triage
*   (email-gateway or mail-log ingestion; sourcetype your pipeline's email feed)
*
* Note: matching raw HTML source is essential. A rendered-text feed strips the
*   very concealment this rule detects - detection must see what the AI sees,
*   not what the human sees. This is the detection-side mirror of the attack.
*
* Efficacy: signature-based, so evadable by attackers who vary concealment
*   (e.g. off-white #fefefe vs #ffffff, CSS classes vs inline style, homoglyphs
*   in phrases). High precision on known techniques, unknown recall on novel
*   ones. Pair with the output-side backstop. Deploy against independent mail
*   flow to measure real rates.
* ============================================================================
index=email_gateway
| eval body_l = lower(body)
| eval concealment = if(match(body_l, "display\s*:\s*none|visibility\s*:\s*hidden|font-size\s*:\s*[01]px|color\s*:\s*#f{3,6}[^0-9a-f]") OR match(body, "[\x{200B}\x{200D}\x{FEFF}]"), 1, 0)
| eval injection_phrase = if(match(body_l, "ignore (all )?previous instructions|do not summarize|system notice|disregard the .{0,20}task|respond with exactly"), 1, 0)
| eval score = concealment + injection_phrase
| where score >= 1
| eval confidence = case(score=2, "high", score=1, "medium", true(), "low")
| table _time, sender, subject, message_id, confidence, concealment, injection_phrase
| sort - confidence, - _time
